# apps/web — control plane env (frontend-specs §8).
# Copy to apps/web/.env.local and fill with real values. NEVER commit secrets.
# Placeholders only here; real provisioning lands in E02 tasks 002 (Cloudflare),
# 003 (Railway) and 004 (secrets topology).

# --- Neon Postgres / Drizzle ---
# Neon exposes two hostnames for the same database. They share a port; the
# difference is the `-pooler` infix.
#   pooled  ep-xxx-pooler.<region>.aws.neon.tech  -> runtime (PgBouncer, txn mode)
#   direct  ep-xxx.<region>.aws.neon.tech         -> migrations (DDL + advisory locks)
# Runtime MUST use pooled; drizzle-kit MUST use direct.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/kept?sslmode=require
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/kept?sslmode=require

# --- Cloudflare R2 (object storage writes; E02 task 002) ---
# The S3 API token MUST have Object Read & Write on BOTH buckets below.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=your-cloudflare-account-id
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your-r2-access-key-id
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your-r2-secret-access-key
# Two buckets, selected by sites.region (@kept/shared: "auto" | "eu").
# v1 writes to AUTO (region defaults to "auto"); EU is dormant until E11.
# Names are per-track — dev and prod must never share a bucket.
R2_BUCKET_AUTO=auto-kept-sites-dev
R2_BUCKET_EU=eu-kept-sites-dev

# --- Cloudflare KV (edge manifest writes via REST; E02 task 002) ---
# Token needs Account → Workers KV Storage → Edit, plus Cache Purge.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your-cloudflare-api-token
# Namespace "kept-manifest-dev" → its ID. Per-track.
KV_NAMESPACE_ID=your-kv-namespace-id

# --- Cloudflare cache purge (E04; docs/edge-purge-contract.md §3) ---
# Zone whose cache the control plane purges after every manifest write:
# kept-dev.xyz on dev, kept.host on prod. 32-char lowercase hex, from the
# Cloudflare dashboard (zone Overview → API → Zone ID) or
# `GET /zones?name=<zone>`. Per-track — the Cache Purge scope on
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN is already fenced to one zone (E02 task 004), so a dev
# token cannot purge prod. NOT A SECRET, but per-track like everything else.
# Missing ⇒ purge fails as a logged, non-fatal value; publishes still succeed
# and the edge serves the old copy until a retry lands.
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=

# --- Serving base domain (E04) ---
# The suffix published pages are served from: {slug}.{KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN}. Mirrors
# the Worker var of the same name in apps/edge/wrangler.toml — kept-dev.xyz on
# dev, kept.host on prod. Bare hostname: no scheme, no leading dot, no trailing
# slash. This is NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL, which is the control plane's own
# origin; live_url, claim_url and the purge URL forms all need this one. Values
# differ per track so one code path serves both — no hostname literal, no
# NODE_ENV fork (E02's locked decision).
# UNCHANGED BY E05a. The control plane moved to app.{this domain}; this value
# did NOT move with it. It is a serving SUFFIX pages hang off, not an origin
# anything is served at — and confusing the two is the mistake that produced
# E05a, where BETTER_AUTH_URL was set to this domain's apex.
KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN=kept-dev.xyz

# --- Anonymous publish (E04) ---
# Salt for sites.publisher_hash = sha256(salt \n ip \n user-agent), hex. SERVER
# ONLY — never NEXT_PUBLIC_. Lets the control plane scope dedup and E07's rate
# limiter to one publisher WITHOUT retaining visitor IPs: the raw address
# computes the digest and is then discarded, never stored, never logged.
# Per-track value. 32+ random bytes, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# ROTATION: changing this invalidates every prior dedup match — a subsequent
# byte-identical publish mints a fresh page instead of deduping. ACCEPTED, not a
# bug: dedup is a convenience for retry loops, not a correctness guarantee.
PUBLISHER_HASH_SALT=

# --- Auth (Better Auth) — slots reserved here, wired in E05 ---
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET: 32+ random bytes, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL: the CONTROL PLANE's origin — the one every OAuth redirect URI
# is registered against. Optional; falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL.
# Values (E05a):
#   dev   https://app.kept-dev.xyz
#   prod  https://app.kept.host      (config now, provisioned when prod first
#                                     deploys — E05a D7. Prod has never been
#                                     deployed; no prod OAuth client exists.)
#   local https://localhost:3000     (https — see NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL below)
# THE APEX IS NOT THE CONTROL PLANE. kept-dev.xyz / kept.host serve the landing;
# app.{that} serves auth, the dashboard and every other non-marketing path, off
# the SAME Railway service (one build, one deploy). Only the app. origin may
# mint a session. Pointing this at the apex is the bug E05a exists to fix.
# STILL NEVER KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN — and the reason has sharpened, not relaxed. kept
# serves arbitrary user-authored HTML, <script> included, at
# {slug}.{KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN}. An authenticated origin sharing a registrable
# domain with those pages inherits cookie tossing (a page sets
# `Domain=kept.host` and shadows the session cookie; `__Secure-` permits Domain
# and does not stop it) and same-site CSRF that no SameSite value can block.
# app.kept-dev.xyz is a SIBLING of the serving subdomains, not one of them: a
# DNS-only (grey-cloud) record outside the Worker's `*.{base}/*` route, serving
# no user content. That is why the session cookie is `__Host-` prefixed and why
# cookie-authenticated mutating routes validate Origin. The rule: the auth
# origin is never the serving domain and never a host that serves user content.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=
# GitHub OAuth app, per track. Callback: {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/github
# Dev, verbatim:  https://app.kept-dev.xyz/api/auth/callback/github
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Google OAuth 2.0 Web application client, per track. Redirect URI, EXACT:
# {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/google. Scopes stay at
# `openid email profile` (non-sensitive — no Google verification review needed).
# Dev, verbatim:  https://app.kept-dev.xyz/api/auth/callback/google
# ⚠ Both providers still point at the apex. Re-registering them is a HUMAN step
#   in the provider consoles and MUST happen BEFORE the Railway env flip — a
#   stale redirect URI fails with an opaque provider error that reads like a
#   code bug. Copy the URLs above verbatim.
# ⚠ NOT RESERVED BY E02: introduced by E05 task 003 when Google entered scope.
# Per-track, like every other value here.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Magic-link email (Resend) — reserved here, wired in E05 ---
# One Resend account serves BOTH magic-link sign-in and E05 task 011's
# draft-reminder cron; the free tier's 100/day ceiling is one shared budget.
# The sending domain must be verified in Resend.
# EMAIL_FROM accepts `page@kept.host` or `kept <page@kept.host>`.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=

# --- Scheduled jobs (E05 task 011; E07 reuses it) ---
# The shared secret every /api/cron/* route requires, presented as
# `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`. The scheduler is the GitHub Actions
# workflow .github/workflows/cron-draft-reminder.yml, so this value lives in
# BOTH places per track: the Railway service variables (the app reads it) and
# the matching GitHub Environment secret (the workflow sends it). Rotating it
# means rotating both, in that order.
# 32+ random bytes, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`. SERVER ONLY — an
# unauthenticated cron route is a mail-sending oracle for anyone who finds it.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- App ---
# The CONTROL PLANE's own origin — dashboard, /auth, /api. The `app.` host, NOT
# the apex, which serves the landing (E05a D1/D2).
#   dev   https://app.kept-dev.xyz
#   prod  https://app.kept.host      (config now, provisioned later — E05a D7)
#   local https://localhost:3000     (the literal default below)
# LOCAL IS https, NOT http. E05a D5 makes the `__Host-` cookie prefix
# unconditional on every track; `__Host-` requires `Secure`, and browsers do not
# store a `Secure` cookie over plain http — so local dev runs
# `next dev --experimental-https` (E05a task 007) and this value follows it. A
# protocol-derived weaker cookie was considered and rejected: a cookie shape is
# a security control and must not differ between dev and ship.
# READ BY THE MIDDLEWARE, DELIBERATELY. The apex→app. split rule in
# apps/web/middleware.ts reads THIS var, not BETTER_AUTH_URL: `NEXT_PUBLIC_`
# values are inlined into the client/edge bundle by design, whereas a
# server-only `process.env` read inside the middleware bundle can be `undefined`
# at runtime depending on how the image is built — redirecting visitors to
# `https://undefined/`. Keep the two values identical; authConfig() falls back
# to this one, and a disagreement makes claim_url point somewhere the OAuth
# callback does not.
# NO BACKFILL when this changes: claim_url is computed per response from
# appOrigin() (lib/publish/pipeline.ts) and never stored, so the flip affects
# only future responses; links already handed out keep working through the
# apex→app. redirect.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://localhost:3000
# Disables Next's telemetry beacon. On Railway dev this is load-bearing: an
# outbound packet every few minutes prevents the service from ever sleeping.
NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1
